<p>This seems similar in some ways to things like SCP-1504. The angle you're trying to take here is unique but I'm not sure it would be enough to carry it conceptually.</p>
<p>If you're continuing with this story, it'll have to lean heavily on the narrative. Nothing else about it strikes me as particularly unique other than you can include some major tension points in the narrative as the Foundation can't actually get rid of him. I'd play it more into the idea of the Foundation's issue being that they're keeping an anomaly, which would be great irony given that keeping anomalies is usually their goal. Try to work in a reason why they can't just lock him away or amnesticize him. I skimmed your sandbox draft and it looks well-written, so maybe you already addressed that, but I figured I ought to mention it here.</p>

