<p>hmm. I'm a little conflicted on this. There's definitely a solid emotional core to this, and I think the way you're wanting to tell it will be effective for that; but on some level it feels&#8230;a little expected maybe?</p>
<p>I have some concerns that the SAR incident reports will be hard to pull off well: it feels like they might struggle to keep a reader on board since they're effectively passive characterisation designed to make us care about the dog, without much like, narratively pulling our interest forward; and ensuring you have enough reports to get a reader to care without having so many that the reader gets bored will potentially be hard to balance.</p>
<p>This said: I think figuring out whether you've got the balance right is gonna be easiest at the drafting stage and so I'm gonna <span style="color: green">greenlight</span> this. I'm also gonna suggest you check out <a href="/wilson-s-wildlife-solutions-hub">Wilson's Wildlife Solutions</a> since this kinda thing feels like it could really easily tie in there.</p>

